prompt:
Read all canonical Stock Research Lab documents and previous audit results first.

Review especially:

- docs/historical-research-protocol.md
- docs/top30-observation-contract.md
- docs/audits/JOB-0006-point-in-time-leakage-audit.md
- docs/audits/JOB-0009-legacy-historical-artifact-audit.md
- docs/experiments/JOB-0008-genuine-telegram-source.md
- src/research_contracts/top30.py
- src/research_contracts/historical_fixture.py
- src/research_contracts/legacy_artifact_inventory.py

TARGET REPOSITORY

D:/Project/research

LEGACY CODE REFERENCE

D:/Project/stock-trading-system-reference

Treat as strictly READ ONLY.

LEGACY DATA REFERENCE

D:/Project/stock

Treat as strictly READ ONLY.

LOCAL SECRET SOURCE

D:/Project/research/.env

May be consumed at runtime only.

Never print, persist, summarize, copy, or expose secret values.

EXISTING TELEGRAM SESSION

D:/Project/stock/telegram_test.session

This is a local legacy session candidate.

CRITICAL SESSION SAFETY RULE

Treat the existing session file as READ ONLY.

Do not allow Telegram/Telethon or any other client to open this original
session file in a mode that may update, migrate, rewrite, lock, journal,
or otherwise mutate it.

If Telegram client operation requires a writable session:

- first create a Git-ignored working COPY under the target repository's
  local session area;
- verify the copy is excluded by Git;
- use only the working copy;
- never modify the original legacy session.

Do not persist session contents or authentication material in research
artifacts.

If the session is unauthorized, stop acquisition and report that fact
without requesting or exposing OTP/2FA credentials.

TARGET HISTORICAL ARTIFACT

Legacy raw file:

D:/Project/stock/data/telegram/daily/2023-09-01_07-36-30_15617.txt

Telegram message ID:

15617

Use the source/channel identity expected by the legacy acquisition code.

Do not guess a channel identifier if it cannot be established from existing
configuration/code without exposing secrets.

OBJECTIVE

Determine how much historical provenance can be repaired for exactly this
one genuine legacy Telegram artifact.

Do NOT generalize to all 782 artifacts in this job.

Do NOT perform future-price/outcome analysis.

Do NOT migrate bulk Telegram data.

Do NOT use current KRX master information.

STEP 1 — PRESERVE LEGACY STATE

Before any Telegram access record:

- legacy raw artifact path;
- raw artifact byte length;
- SHA-256 of current legacy bytes;
- legacy processed-manifest linkage if present;
- stored manifest hash if present;
- correction-registry linkage if any;
- legacy session path/existence;
- legacy session file size and SHA-256 for mutation detection.

Do not read or expose session contents.

After Telegram access, verify the ORIGINAL legacy session SHA-256 and size
are unchanged.

Failure of this invariant is HIGH severity and must stop the experiment.

STEP 2 — AUTHORIZATION CHECK

Using a safe working session copy if necessary:

- determine whether the existing Telegram session is authorized;
- do not expose account identity beyond what is necessary;
- do not print phone number, authentication token, API hash, or other
  sensitive account data.

If unauthorized:

- record AUTHORIZATION_BLOCKED;
- do not attempt interactive login;
- do not fabricate provenance;
- stop the network portion safely.

STEP 3 — RETRIEVE EXACT MESSAGE

If authorized, retrieve exactly Telegram message ID 15617.

Do not bulk-download history.

Record non-secret Telegram metadata where available:

- message ID;
- channel/chat stable identifier suitable for provenance;
- message timestamp;
- edit timestamp or explicit none/unknown;
- retrieval timestamp;
- deletion/unavailability state if observable;
- message text/content needed for byte comparison.

Do not infer revision history that Telegram does not expose.

STEP 4 — CONTENT COMPARISON

Compare the legacy local artifact with the currently retrieved Telegram
message.

First determine how the legacy acquisition code serialized Telegram content.

Do not assume the local .txt bytes equal raw Telegram API bytes if the legacy
collector added metadata, line endings, headers, encoding changes, or
normalization.

Produce explicit comparison levels:

1. RAW_FILE_BYTES
2. LEGACY_SERIALIZED_MESSAGE_CONTENT
3. CURRENT_TELEGRAM_MESSAGE_CONTENT

Where deterministic reconstruction is possible, compare canonical serialized
content using the exact legacy serialization rules.

Report:

- exact match;
- canonical match;
- mismatch;
- comparison impossible.

Never silently normalize until content matches.

Document every normalization/transformation used.

STEP 5 — TIMESTAMP / AVAILABILITY ANALYSIS

Keep these concepts separate:

SOURCE_TIMESTAMP
Telegram message date.

EDIT_TIMESTAMP
Telegram edit date if exposed.

LEGACY_FILE_TIMESTAMP
Filesystem timestamp, which is NOT automatically historical evidence.

RETRIEVAL_TIMESTAMP
Current JOB-0010 acquisition time.

HISTORICAL_AVAILABILITY
What can actually be proven about cutoff visibility.

The intended historical cutoff for this artifact should be derived
conservatively from the original Top30 report/source semantics.

Do not assume:

message date == immutable content availability.

If edit_timestamp exists after the intended cutoff:

- current message content must NOT be considered cutoff-visible without
  independent evidence;
- classify accordingly.

If edit_timestamp is absent:

- do not overclaim that no historical edit ever occurred unless Telegram
  semantics actually support that conclusion.

STEP 6 — EVIDENCE CLASSIFICATION

Classify the repaired artifact into exactly one:

A — CUTOFF_VERIFIED

Strong evidence supports source identity, message identity, content, and
availability at the historical cutoff.

B — SOURCE_VERIFIED_CONTENT_STRONGLY_SUPPORTED

Telegram source/message/timestamp are verified and current content matches
the independently preserved legacy artifact, but immutable historical
byte-level availability cannot be proven completely.

C — SOURCE_VERIFIED_CONTENT_UNCERTAIN

Source/message identity is verified but content or edit history leaves
material uncertainty.

D — LOCAL_ONLY_PROVENANCE

Telegram verification could not be completed; only the legacy local artifact
is available.

E — CONTRADICTED

Telegram metadata/content conflicts materially with the legacy artifact.

Be conservative.

A classification requires actual evidence, not inference from filenames.

STEP 7 — PROVENANCE REPAIR ARTIFACT

Create a small deterministic provenance-repair record in the target repository.

It may reference the legacy artifact but must not modify or duplicate the
bulk legacy dataset.

Bind at minimum:

- repair record ID/version;
- legacy artifact path;
- legacy current-audit SHA-256;
- legacy processed-manifest linkage;
- Telegram message ID;
- Telegram source/channel identifier when verified;
- source timestamp;
- edit timestamp;
- retrieval timestamp;
- comparison method;
- comparison result;
- evidence classification;
- availability interpretation;
- provenance limitations;
- repair-record digest.

Clearly label newly calculated hashes as current/repaired audit evidence,
not acquisition-time historical hashes.

STEP 8 — NEW PIPELINE COMPATIBILITY

Determine whether this repaired evidence is sufficient to create a genuine
JOB-0007-style fixture manifest.

If classification/evidence permits, create only the minimal real fixture
manifest needed for the next experiment.

Do NOT create an AssessmentPayload or frozen assessment in this job unless
the evidence meets the existing point-in-time contract without weakening
JOB-0006 protections.

Prefer stopping before assessment rather than overstating evidence.

STEP 9 — CORRECTION BOUNDARY

Check whether message 15617 has any entry in:

D:/Project/stock/data/manual/telegram/top30_corrections.csv

If none, record none.

If one exists, keep it strictly separate as retrospective correction metadata.

Never alter raw content using correction data.

STEP 10 — TESTS

Add focused tests proving at minimum:

1. original legacy session cannot be modified by the acquisition adapter;
2. working session copies remain Git-ignored;
3. original session size/hash mutation detection works;
4. credential/session contents are not persisted;
5. legacy raw artifact digest is deterministic;
6. legacy processed-manifest linkage is validated when available;
7. Telegram source/message identity remains explicit;
8. source/edit/retrieval timestamps remain distinct;
9. no-edit and unknown-edit semantics are not overstated;
10. comparison transformations are explicit;
11. mismatch cannot be converted into a match by undocumented normalization;
12. repaired provenance classification is explicit;
13. current audit digest cannot masquerade as acquisition-time digest;
14. retrospective correction data remains separate;
15. existing JOB-0006 leakage tests still pass;
16. existing JOB-0007 write-once tests still pass;
17. JOB-0009 provenance safeguards still pass.

NO OUTCOME DATA

Do not access or attach:

- future OHLCV;
- +5/+10/+20/+60 returns;
- sector outcomes;
- success/failure labels;
- future KRX identity.

DOCUMENTATION

Create:

docs/experiments/JOB-0010-telegram-provenance-repair.md

Document:

- target artifact;
- session handling method;
- authorization result;
- Telegram metadata result;
- legacy serialization reconstruction;
- content comparison;
- timestamp semantics;
- availability analysis;
- evidence classification;
- provenance-repair record;
- whether a genuine fixture manifest was permitted;
- limitations.

READINESS VERDICT

End with exactly one:

REAL_FIXTURE_PROVENANCE_READY
REAL_FIXTURE_PROVENANCE_READY_WITH_RESTRICTIONS
PROVENANCE_STILL_INSUFFICIENT
AUTHORIZATION_BLOCKED
PROVENANCE_CONTRADICTED

This verdict applies only to this single message and the next small
historical fixture step.

It does NOT authorize bulk repair, outcome joining, or the three-year study.

QUALITY

Run the complete pytest suite.
Run git diff --check.

Do not commit or push.

FINAL RESPONSE

Report:

- requested model tier;
- actual model if telemetry exposes it;
- target message ID;
- authorization status;
- original legacy session mutation check;
- Telegram source timestamp;
- Telegram edit timestamp status;
- retrieval timestamp;
- legacy artifact SHA-256;
- legacy manifest hash match/mismatch;
- content comparison result;
- evidence classification;
- correction linkage;
- whether a genuine fixture manifest was allowed;
- tests;
- readiness verdict;
- residual risks;
- recommended next job.

Never expose credentials, phone numbers, session contents, or authentication
tokens.
